+3.  **De-mapping (Runtime):** When a ray is processed, fetch the voxel from bank $k$. Compute the de-mapped position index $D_z$ using Eq. 3 to determine the voxel's relative position along the ray [S1, book].
 4.  **Beam Scanning Optimization:** During viewing, as the system scans from one beam to the next, the bank index (distance of a voxel along the beam from the viewing position) is incremented or decremented by 1 modulo $n$. This change is controlled locally by the local address unit of the bank, avoiding the need to re-evaluate the skewing formula (Eq. 1 or 4) for every voxel during the time-consuming viewing process [S1, book].
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Directional Coverage:** The linear skewing scheme (Eq. 4) only guarantees conflict-free access for the "desired directions" specified during parameter selection. Accessing a direction outside this set may result in bank conflicts [S1, book].
-*   **Modulus Selection:** The choice of $n'$ is critical. It must be compatible with the coefficients $a, b, c$ to avoid collisions. For example, $n'=517$ is specifically cited for 26 directions [S1, book].
-*   **Precision:** The de-mapping formula (Eq. 3) relies on modular arithmetic. Incorrect implementation of the modulo operator (e.g., handling negative numbers) will lead to incorrect spatial reconstruction [S1, book].
